{"id": 17, "ts": 1780463763.9426527, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "emily_brooks", "message": "I agree with James that we need to look beyond Candidate\u202fB\u2019s technical strengths and examine the interpersonal side more closely. While B\u2019s conscientiousness and stress handling are solid, we still have no concrete evidence of his crew\u2011cooperation or communication, and the notes flag a \u201cgrumpy\u201d and occasionally uncooperative tone.\nTurning to Candidate\u202fD, the facts I have show he \u201ctakes responsibility seriously,\u201d \u201cconcentrates very well,\u201d and \u201csolves problems extremely well,\u201d all of which map directly to decision quality, attention to detail, and responsibility\u2014core safety criteria for long\u2011distance flights. The only concerns are his \u201cmoody\u201d demeanor and being viewed as a \u201cloner,\u201d which could affect crew dynamics, but these are comparable to B\u2019s attitude issues and, unlike B, we have clear evidence of D\u2019s responsibility and problem\u2011solving ability.\nWe also need to give a quick look at the still\u2011under\u2011discussed candidates. Candidate\u202fA can \u201canticipate dangerous situations,\u201d has \u201cexcellent spatial vision,\u201d and shows \u201cvery good leadership qualities,\u201d but is described as \u201cunfriendly\u201d and has unhealthy habits, raising red flags for crew cooperation and personal reliability. Candidate\u202fC makes \u201ccorrect decisions quickly\u201d and has \u201cexcellent attention skills,\u201d yet he \u201chas difficulty communicating ideas,\u201d is \u201cregarded as egocentric,\u201d and is \u201cnot very willing to further his education,\u201d which undermine both crew cooperation and adaptability.\nGiven the balance of concrete safety\u2011critical strengths versus interpersonal risks, I think Candidate\u202fD currently offers the strongest overall fit, provided we address his temperament through